#passage(("In the Quran, in chapter:9 of the Quran, verse:30, it does not say that Jews worship Ezra as God. This is not the wording of the verse. Allah says in the Quran (atTawba9:30) وَقَالَتِ ٱلْيَهُودُ عُزَيْرٌ ٱبْنُ ٱللَّهِ (And the Jews said that Ezra -or 'Uzair- is the son of God) وَقَالَتِ ٱلنَّصَـٰرَى ٱلْمَسِيحُ ٱبْنُ ٱللَّهِ (And the Christians said that Jesus is the son of God). So the verse there doesn't say anything about worship. It says their claim that he is the son of God. That is the claim that they make. Yes, there is a Hadith about the worship, I believe, in Sahih Muslim, but this is not what the verse is saying. When you claim that the Quran says that the Jewish people worship Ezra as the son of God, that's not what the Quran is saying.", "He says that we don't find anything about this in Torah, we don't find anything about this in the Talmud. I'm going to tell you what is present in the Torah and the Talmud. First, who's 'Uzair, that Quran talks about, that the Jews said: عُزَيْرٌ ٱبْنُ ٱللَّهِ (Uzair is the son of Allah)? If you remove the vowels and you turn the word into Hebrew, it's going to be Ezra. So Ezra is a prophetic figure mentioned in the Old Testament, and mentioned in the Bible generally. He's well known by both Christians and Jewish people. Now, was Ezra the son of God in the Bible? Did the Jewish people say that? Did the Christians say that? Did anybody say that? So what we do? We do research!", "First, I'm going to talk about something called the Book of Malachi. What does the word מַלְאָכִי (Malachi) mean? The word Malachi in Hebrew means \"my angel\", like مَلَكي, Malachi in Arabic, means \"my angel\". So we have a book called the Book of Malachi. You have some people saying: Malachi is a name and it's actually a person. And that is a very weak argument for many many reasons. There is no evidence at all that this word Malachi is used as a proper noun. It's not a common custom for Jewish people to name their sons as \"my angel\". That doesn't happen. And we already have evidences to show that Malachi there is not a name, but Malachi actually means what the word means. It means \"my angel\". So there's a prophet there, a person mentions there, and this person is called \"the angel\", the angel of God, the angel of Yahweh.", "In the Book of Malachi itself, in chapter2:7, it's translated in NIV and other things as the \"Messenger from God\". But in Hebrew, it says Malach (angel), but they're mistranslating it. So it says the angel of Yahweh. So this book here is about a person who's explicitly called \"the angel of Yahweh\". And you have church fathers, for example, who believe that Malachi was a real angel, like Origen. Origen believed that Malachi was a real angel. And you have many others believing that.", "So we have here Book of Malachi, the Book of my angel. We have a person here who's called explicitly in the book itself the angel of God. So who's this person who's called the angel of God? This person is EZRA. So here we're establishing -this is the first point of the argument- that the Book of Malachi is about Ezra, and Ezra is called the angel of Yahweh, the angel of God. How can we establish that? If you go to Talmud, in Megillah 15a, and you read, it will say: (Rabbi Yehoshua ben Korha said: Malachi is [in fact] Ezra.) אמר רבי יהושע בן קרחה: מלאכי זה עזרא. And then he's going to quote someone else: Rav Nahman Said: It stands to reason that indeed, they are one and the same person. Ezra and Malachi, they are one and the same individual.", "And then he says, as is stated in Malachi's prophecy -so he gives the reason why-: \"Judah has dealt treacherously, and a disgusting thing has been done in Israel and in Jerusalem,\" \"For Judah has profaned the sanctity of the Lord which he loved, and has married the daughter of a strange God\". So this is mentioned in Malachi2:11. And then this is what the Rabbi is saying: \"Who is the one who removed the foreign women who were married to the Jews?\" \"It was Ezra\"! And then he quotes: \"as it was written, the son of Jehiel, one of the sons of Elam said: \"And we have married foreign women of the people of the land\" (Ezra10:2). It therefore appears that Malachi was one of Ezra's names.\" So what do we have here in the Talmud? Explicitly mentioned by rabbis, different rabbis. And they give evidence; they give here two different verses, which shows that Ezra was Malachi. So Ezra was called the angel of God.", "Is that it? No, but look how Jews even glorify Ezra. If you go to Sanhedrin in the Talmud, 21b:24, you will see one of the rabbis says: \"Ezra was suitable, given his greatness, for the Torah to be given by him to the Jewish people, had Moses not come first\". So if Moses didn't come, he would even give the Torah to Ezra. This is how much glorification these individuals give to Ezra.", "But now the point is very simple. They say Ezra is an angel; he's called the angel of the Lord, as I already quoted. He has the book of Malachi; it's called Malachi, the title given to him, the angel of the Lord. So Ezra is called an angel of God. Are the angels sons of God? Yes, the angels are sons of God, according to the book of Job, chapter:1, verse:6. It mentions how the angels are explicitly called sons of God, and there are other instances in the Bible, and many scholars who say this; it's a common belief. So essentially what did we prove here? We proved from the Torah and the Talmud and the New Testament, even church fathers' beliefs, that Ezra is the son of God, as the Quran states. This is something that Jewish rabbis believe in, and this is something that is mentioned in the Talmud. This is a claim of the Quran! The Quran is saying that the Jews said that he was the son of God. They called him the angel of God, and angels are the sons of God. We have proved the point.", "Now my question would be, how did Prophet Muhammadﷺ know that? I'm here doing reading, and bringing different scriptures and opinions of