=== 86.3 The Year of the Elephant
// NODE P10.C05.S06
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR006:28-104 | Farid Responds | 11
#passage(("I give a background for Surat al-Fil, the chapter of the elephant, from both Muslim sources and non-Muslim sources. The most important thing to do when speaking about this specific matter is to establish its historicity. I don't want to go into too much detail in regards to the specifics of the elephant, since that is a topic that is usually covered in the Sira itself, but I feel a need to address some matters regarding the historicity of the event.", "Quraysh would speak about the year of the elephant, and they would say things like, \"This occurred this many years after the year of the elephant.\" Hakim bin Hizam said, \"I was born before the year of the elephant by 13 years.\" So, what this was was [Am al-Fil], the year of the elephant. [The Quran] preserves the event in chapter 105, pointing out the people of the elephant came, their plans were ruined, and they were attacked by birds that dropped stones at them, annihilating them. The details of this event are found in the Sira, and despite the fact that this event isn't even connected to the prophet, peace be upon him, directly, it does establish that the Kaaba was a sacred place, and that a miracle did occur in the year 570 CE.", "If no such thing occurred, then firstly, we would not have the Arabs referring to that year as the year of the elephant in the first place, since the least that we attain from this specific name is that something major occurred on that year that had to do with an elephant. Also, if the information contained in the verses were false, the Meccans would have denied it, causing the people to reject Islam altogether, despite the events predating Islam by only 40 years. The elderly would have witnessed the period. They would be denying it if anything was incorrect. So, from an outsider's perspective, while this isn't direct proof for Islam, it is proof that something miraculous did occur.", "While there isn't much about this event from non-Muslim historians, since it involves the history of the Hijaz, we are lucky enough to have some information about the events that led up to it in terms of the Abyssinian involvement in the region. Procopius, in chapter 20 of the first section of his history, mentions that the Himyarites were Jews that had oppressed the Christians of Yemen. The king of Abyssinia, Hellestheaeus, sent a fleet of ships, conquered the land, and had their king killed. He then had a king placed there named Asimahis, who was more like a representative, and ordered him to pay a tribute to the Abyssinians once a year. Asimahis, by the way, in Arabic would be Asumafah.", "The Abyssinian army that was sent to Yemen, though, was full of slaves, and decided to stay in the fertile Yemeni lands. So, they deposed of Asumafah. They imprisoned him, and they placed Abraha in his place. Abraha was originally a slave of a Roman citizen who engaged in shipping. The Abyssinian king, Hellestheaeus, sent 3,000 men under one of his commanders to take over Yemen once again. However, the men he sent, after getting to Yemen, decided to make it their home. So, they killed their commander and sided with Abraha. Helesthaeus then sent another army, but it was defeated by Abraha's army. And with that, Abraha's power over the region became well established. And we find the influence of the Abyssinians upon the region to the extent that we even find a famous Yemeni companion, Abu Hurayrah, in the Hadith speaking in the Abyssinian tongue.", "Now, these events seem to have occurred slightly before the year 530 CE, four decades before the year of the elephant. Now, this may sound like a long time ago, but it actually matches with what historians have mentioned. Ad-Dinawari mentions in his al-Akhbar al-Tiwal that Abraha was in power for 40 years. This is also the length provided by Sibt ibn al-Jawzi, who doesn't seem to be taking from Ad-Dinawari. Wallahu a'lam.", "By the way, the narrative mentioned by Ibn Ishaq mentions some similarities to what Procopius mentions, including a report in which Abraha duels with a leader that was sent by Helesthaeus, where he was able to treacherously kill him with the help of a servant. Ibn Ishaq adds that Helesthaeus was angered by the actions of Abraha and swore to step onto his land and cut the forelock of Abraha, implying capturing him. Abraha then cut his own hair, placed it into a pouch with some sand from Yemen. Then he sent it over to Helesthaeus in order for Helesthaeus's oath to be fulfilled in an indirect way, as a way to appease him. So, Abraha still recognized Helesthaeus as king, and so [Helesthaeus] was appeased.", "In regards to what exactly led Abraha's expedition towards the Kaaba, all the reports differ. However, the commonly mentioned report is that Abraha had built a house of worship to compete with the Kaaba, and the man from Kinanah relieved himself there. When Abraha found out, he decided to destroy the Kaaba.", "The event is not mentioned only by the Quran, but by pre-Islamic poets who have alluded to it as well, as we find in the poetry that was preserved by Ibn Ishaq. For example, it is alluded to in the poetry of Abdullah [ibn al-Ziba'ra], who was initially an infamous enemy of Islam. We also find Safwan bin Umayya referring to Allah subhanahu wa ta'ala as he who blocked the elephant. Muhammad bin Habib al-Baghdadi also mentions a couple of examples of poets that composed poetry about the attack.", "Furthermore, some of those that took part in the expedition remained in Mecca long after the events. Aisha herself narrates that she herself saw the caretaker of the elephant as well as its trainer in Mecca. She mentions that both were blind and both were begging for food. Both would be quite old in age by then, perhaps in their 70s or 80s. The report by Ibn Ishaq is reliable as well, and it would suggest that not everyone that participated in the expedition was killed, as some would assume. Qubath bin Ashyam, who was older than the prophet, peace be upon him, states in [Tarikh] Khalifa that he is older than the prophet, peace be upon him, and that he recalls standing with his mother before the [dung] of the elephant. He remembers it when he was a child.", "Ibn Ishaq said concerning the whole event that when Allah repelled Abraha's army from Mecca and afflicted them with what he had afflicted them of punishment, the Arabs held the Quraysh in even greater esteem. They said, \"They are the people of Allah. Allah fought in their behalf and spared them the burden of the enemy.\" So, they composed poems about that, mentioning therein what God did to the people of Habesha.",), "Farid Responds, “Non-Islamic Miracle but Proof for Islam!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sDjJwnEKMJM", ((117, "1:57–2:32"), (154, "2:34–3:21"), (204, "3:24–4:14"), (254, "4:14–5:07"), (309, "5:09–6:12"), (375, "6:15–6:46"), (407, "6:47–7:43"), (467, "7:47–8:10"), (491, "8:11–8:45"), (531, "8:51–9:50"), (594, "9:54–10:22"),))

